DDEEFFAAUULLTT SSCCHHEEMMEE FFIILLEESS FFOORRMMAATT
       The following statement is recognized:

          nnaammee ccoolloorr--sseeqquueennccee

       The nnaammee is a logical name of color sequence (for example "error"). The
       names are specific to the utilities. For more details always see the
       CCOOLLOORRSS section in the man page for the utility.

       The ccoolloorr--sseeqquueennccee is a color name, ASCII color sequences or escape
       sequences.

   CCoolloorr nnaammeess
       black, blink, blue, bold, brown, cyan, darkgray, gray, green,
       halfbright, lightblue, lightcyan, lightgray, lightgreen, lightmagenta,
       lightred, magenta, red, reset, reverse, and yellow.

   AANNSSII ccoolloorr sseeqquueenncceess
       The color sequences are composed of sequences of numbers separated by
       semicolons. The most common codes are:

          allbox tab(:); lt lt. T{

          0 T}:T{

          to restore default color T} T{

          1 T}:T{

          for brighter colors T} T{

          4 T}:T{

          for underlined text T} T{

          5 T}:T{

          for flashing text T} T{

          30 T}:T{

          for black foreground T} T{

          31 T}:T{

          for red foreground T} T{

          32 T}:T{

          for green foreground T} T{

          33 T}:T{

          for yellow (or brown) foreground T} T{

          34 T}:T{

          for blue foreground T} T{

          35 T}:T{

          for purple foreground T} T{

          36 T}:T{

          for cyan foreground T} T{

          37 T}:T{

          for white (or gray) foreground T} T{

          40 T}:T{

          for black background T} T{

          41 T}:T{

          for red background T} T{

          42 T}:T{

          for green background T} T{

          43 T}:T{

          for yellow (or brown) background T} T{

          44 T}:T{

          for blue background T} T{

          45 T}:T{

          for purple background T} T{

          46 T}:T{

          for cyan background T} T{

          47 T}:T{

          for white (or gray) background T}


   EEssccaappee sseeqquueenncceess
       To specify control or blank characters in the color sequences, C-style
       \-escaped notation can be used:

          allbox tab(:); lt lt. T{

          \\aa T}:T{

          Bell (ASCII 7) T} T{

          \\bb T}:T{

          Backspace (ASCII 8) T} T{

          \\ee T}:T{

          Escape (ASCII 27) T} T{

          \\ff T}:T{

          Form feed (ASCII 12) T} T{

          \\nn T}:T{

          Newline (ASCII 10) T} T{

          \\rr T}:T{

          Carriage Return (ASCII 13) T} T{

          \\tt T}:T{

          Tab (ASCII 9) T} T{

          \\vv T}:T{

          Vertical Tab (ASCII 11) T} T{

          \\?? T}:T{

          Delete (ASCII 127) T} T{

          \\__ T}:T{

          Space T} T{

          \\\\ T}:T{

          Backslash (\) T} T{

          \\^^ T}:T{

          Caret (^) T} T{

          \\## T}:T{

          Hash mark (#) T}


       Please note that escapes are necessary to enter a space, backslash,
       caret, or any control character anywhere in the string, as well as a
       hash mark as the first character.

       For example, to use a red background for alert messages in the output
       of ddmmeessgg(1), use:

          eecchhoo ''aalleerrtt 3377;;4411'' >>>> //eettcc//tteerrmmiinnaall--ccoolloorrss..dd//ddmmeessgg..sscchheemmee

   CCoommmmeennttss
       Lines where the first non-blank character is a # (hash) are ignored.
       Any other use of the hash character is not interpreted as introducing a
       comment.

